  "Number": 38,
  "Question": "If \\(4x+\\sqrt{2x}=1,\\) then \\(x:\\)",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "is an integer",
    "B": "is fractional",
    "C": "is irrational",
    "D": "is imaginary",
    "E": "may have two different values"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "B",
  "Solution": "The square root requires \\(x\\ge0\\). The two terms must add to \\(1\\), so try making each term \\(1/2\\). Setting \\(4x=1/2\\) gives \\(x=1/8\\), and this also makes\n\\[\n\\sqrt{2x}=\\sqrt{\\frac14}=\\frac12.\n\\]\nThus \\(x=1/8\\) satisfies the equation.\n\nTo prove that it is the only solution, consider the other allowed values. If \\(0\\le x<1/8\\), then \\(4x<1/2\\) and \\(\\sqrt{2x}<1/2\\), so their sum is less than \\(1\\). If \\(x>1/8\\), both terms are greater than \\(1/2\\), so their sum is greater than \\(1\\). These cases exclude every other real value.\n\nTherefore \\(x=\\boxed{1/8}\\), a rational number that is not an integer. It is fractional, choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{B}}\\).",
